const metaFields = [{"label": "CLIENT", "value": "Ocean Conservation Administration, Ocean Affairs Council / National Museum of Natural Science"}, {"label": "LOCATION", "value": "National Museum of Natural Science, Taichung, Taiwan"}, {"label": "YEAR", "value": "2024"}, {"label": "AREA", "value": "Approx. 330㎡ interior + a semi-open pool area"}, {"label": "CATEGORY", "value": "Exhibition Design, Interior Design, Construction"}, {"label": "ROLE", "value": "Design-Build General Contractor"}];
const bodyHtml = "<p>The Central Taiwan Marine Conservation Education Center takes the Indo-Pacific humpback dolphin and the coastal ecology of central Taiwan as its narrative core, converting a former restaurant space into a center that combines exhibition, education, office, and public-event functions. It opened in September 2024 on the second floor of the Earth Environment Hall at the National Museum of Natural Science, commissioned by the Ocean Conservation Administration.</p>\n<p>Letsuan Design served as design-build general contractor, integrating overall spatial planning, exhibition design and fabrication, a purpose-built science classroom, accessibility upgrades, and mechanical/electrical improvements, so that the knowledge of marine conservation extends from the exhibition content into the space itself. Built around \"exhibit plus education,\" the center brings together an education area, offices, exhibition galleries, a science classroom, and the semi-open Longhu Pond, letting visitors move from looking to interacting, learning, and discussing.</p>\n<h2>Why This Center, Why Now</h2>\n<p>This is the third marine conservation education center under the Ocean Conservation Administration's \"Homage to the Sea\" program, following ones in Tainan and Penghu. Central Taiwan's coast is critical habitat for the critically endangered Indo-Pacific humpback dolphin and is threaded with important wetlands. Because these coastlines, estuaries, and wetlands are interconnected — and closely woven into people's daily lives — the exhibition starts with the dolphin and gradually widens outward to habitat, estuary, and wetland, before returning to the relationship between people and the sea, so visitors understand that the dolphin's survival is inseparable from the health of the wider marine environment.</p>\n<h2>A Messenger From the Sea</h2>\n<p>The exhibition is built around two threads — critical marine wildlife habitat and wetland conservation — narrated through a \"messenger from the sea\" device: visitors follow the dolphin through the waters it lives in, starting from environmental change, learning its life history and habitat, moving into the estuaries and wetlands of central Taiwan's coast, and arriving finally at the relationship between people and the ocean. The journey ends by leaving room to think about how conservation and development might be balanced, rather than rushing to a single answer.</p>\n<p>The spatial perspective design echoes that narrative rhythm: the entrance opens with a near-aerial, bird's-eye view that establishes the overall environment, then gradually shifts to eye-level, upward, and distant views as visitors move deeper in. The scale of what's being seen narrows in step, from a vast expanse of ocean down to wetlands, estuaries, and individual creatures, building an understanding of habitat through spatial experience at different scales.</p>\n<h2>Exhibits and Interactive Devices</h2>\n<p>The entrance establishes a light, clean, and layered first impression, using a tiered display window to bring the imagery of the ocean and its habitats indoors. The midsection of the gallery combines multimedia displays with hands-on teaching aids, so that more abstract knowledge about wetlands, estuaries, and marine ecology can be understood through interaction. At the end of the route, a multi-purpose stepped activity area and reading corner support group visits, video screenings, class activities, and independent reading, alongside an interactive station where visitors can draw their own white dolphin.</p>\n<h2>Built to Teach</h2>\n<p>The spatial plan also incorporates learning for older adults, bilingual signage, assistive technology, and accessible design, so visitors of different ages, backgrounds, and needs can each find a way to read and learn that works for them. The science classroom extends the exhibition content into real coursework — hands-on experiments, exploratory activities, and \"from fish to table\" food education — so knowledge gained in the galleries carries through into class learning about the relationship between the marine environment and daily life.</p>\n<h2>An Identity Drawn From Refracted Light</h2>\n<p>The overall visual identity is built on sans-serif and rounded typefaces, with subtle stroke variations giving it a clean but approachable, neutral character. The logo's core motif is an arching line drawn from the flow of the ocean and the way sunlight refracts through the water's surface, threading together three themes: marine ecology, forest-river-estuary, and conservation. Color runs on luminous gradients that echo how natural light shifts as it passes through water; icons for each sub-theme throughout the gallery share the same arc motif, keeping the different exhibit units visually consistent and helping visitors track the thematic thread and the route.</p>\n<h2>Turning a Restaurant Into a Museum</h2>\n<p>This space was originally a restaurant, and converting it into a space that meets museum standards for exhibition and education meant the construction team first had to resolve structural, waterproofing, mechanical/electrical, fire-safety, accessibility, and site-logistics issues left behind after the existing space was stripped out — all preconditions before any exhibition content could move in. Once the former restaurant was demolished down to the structural slab, a level difference of more than 7 centimeters appeared between the interior and the outside corridor, and part of the floor slab was damaged during demolition. After the crew moved in, a leak was also found at a building expansion joint; repeated investigation traced the source not to the joint itself, but to the contact surface of a floor drain outside a planter. The floor-level difference was corrected by the general contractor, and the leak was repaired at its source, giving the subsequent interior work a stable foundation.</p>\n<p>The office area sits directly next to the central air-conditioning plant room, so beyond spatial layout, the noise and fire-safety conditions generated by the plant room also had to be addressed. Rock wool was installed as a fire-resistant material, with additional soundproofing added on the plant-room side to reduce the vibration and noise from equipment operation, keeping the office area a workable environment.</p>\n<p>Earlier demolition had cut wall outlets and sections of wiring, leaving the original circuits unusable, so the electrical system across the whole area had to be rewired from scratch. A new pantry area in the office required newly routed water supply and drainage lines; the science classroom, built to host \"fish to table\" food-education classes, needed an independent circuit for induction cooktops along with matching plumbing. Because the museum had to stay open throughout, moving the cement mortar needed for floor reconstruction through the museum's public circulation would have directly affected visitors — so the crew instead removed a glass window upstairs and hoisted materials up from outside using a winch, keeping the museum's existing gallery circulation completely unaffected by construction.</p>\n<h2>An Accessibility Review That Touched the Whole Building</h2>\n<p>Once the project applied for a change-of-use permit and submitted its interior renovation for review, accessibility became a major thread running through the entire construction process. The existing men's restroom was reconfigured, with the entrance repositioned, non-compliant sinks and urinals removed, fixture counts and spacing reorganized, and a child-height urinal and accessible grab bars added. The accessible restroom's existing opening was too narrow, requiring the door frame and wall to be reworked; that work was scheduled on the museum's closed days, using heavy equipment to cut out and rebuild the existing frame and wall so construction never disrupted the museum's regular visiting hours. The review also extended to accessible parking, the elevator, and floor signage. The riser height and tread depth of an existing straight staircase couldn't be brought into compliance without compromising the existing structure, so a wheelchair lift was installed instead to resolve vertical accessibility.</p>\n<h2>Five Exhibit Zones</h2>\n<p>The gallery is organized into five exhibit zones. \"Satoumi and Estuary Ecology\" draws on the concept of satoumi to explain how the coast and waters of western central Taiwan are both a place where people live and critical habitat for the Indo-Pacific humpback dolphin and the finless porpoise. The \"Taiwan Cetacean Information Wall\" combines a cetacean-sighting map, a seasonal white-dolphin monitoring report, and a finless-porpoise stranding report, updated quarterly with cetacean data from the waters around Taiwan. \"Wetland Ecology\" uses models to depict central Taiwan's important wetlands, alongside stranded-dolphin specimens, wetland crab specimens, and specimens of migratory birds and raptors. \"Dolphins of Critical Habitat\" is the center's key exhibit unit — a large tabletop display presenting the life-history knowledge of the humpback dolphin and the finless porpoise, with a 1:1 dolphin model and skeletal specimens suspended above it. \"Longhu Pond: Three Named White Dolphins\" builds a water curtain above the pond, where three white dolphin models — named after their Ocean Conservation Administration tracking numbers, OCA001 \"Camellia,\" OCA004 \"Fountain,\" and OCA008 \"Strawberry Milk\" — give physical form to the center's \"messenger from the sea\" narrative.</p>\n<p>This project spanned the conversion of an existing restaurant, exhibition narrative, visual identity, interactive education, accessibility, and mechanical/electrical and construction work, requiring multiple disciplines to move in sync before a marine conservation education center could actually operate. Working from the integration of space and exhibition, Letsuan Design turned the relationship between the Indo-Pacific humpback dolphin, wetlands, estuaries, and human life into a space that can be watched, read, operated, and experienced.</p>";
